agentops:index/selfHostedCatalogDeployme.description:
Deploys a curated worker from the worker catalog into **your own** cluster. The control plane validates the catalog entry's credentials and integrations, registers a self-hosted agent and mints its worker token — it does not run anything. Feed <span pulumi-lang-nodejs="`token`" pulumi-lang-dotnet="`Token`" pulumi-lang-go="`token`" pulumi-lang-python="`token`" pulumi-lang-yaml="`token`" pulumi-lang-java="`token`" pulumi-lang-hcl="`token`">`token`</span> into a <span pulumi-lang-nodejs="`kubernetesSecret`" pulumi-lang-dotnet="`KubernetesSecret`" pulumi-lang-go="`kubernetesSecret`" pulumi-lang-python="`kubernetes_secret`" pulumi-lang-yaml="`kubernetesSecret`" pulumi-lang-java="`kubernetesSecret`" pulumi-lang-hcl="`kubernetes_secret`">`kubernetesSecret`</span> and install the catalog entry's image with the `agentops-agent-base` chart to bring the worker online.

Use <span pulumi-lang-nodejs="`agentops.WorkerCatalogDeployment`" pulumi-lang-dotnet="`agentops.WorkerCatalogDeployment`" pulumi-lang-go="`WorkerCatalogDeployment`" pulumi-lang-python="`WorkerCatalogDeployment`" pulumi-lang-yaml="`agentops.WorkerCatalogDeployment`" pulumi-lang-java="`agentops.WorkerCatalogDeployment`" pulumi-lang-hcl="`agentops_worker_catalog_deployment`">`agentops.WorkerCatalogDeployment`</span> instead when the control plane should host the worker for you; that variant generates a git repository and exposes `repo_*` attributes, while this one exposes the token you need to run the worker yourself.

Unlike <span pulumi-lang-nodejs="`agentops.Agent`" pulumi-lang-dotnet="`agentops.Agent`" pulumi-lang-go="`Agent`" pulumi-lang-python="`Agent`" pulumi-lang-yaml="`agentops.Agent`" pulumi-lang-java="`agentops.Agent`" pulumi-lang-hcl="`agentops_agent`">`agentops.Agent`</span>, this endpoint returns no rendered Helm values: the chart coordinates come from the catalog entry (see the <span pulumi-lang-nodejs="`agentops.getWorkerCatalog`" pulumi-lang-dotnet="`agentops.getWorkerCatalog`" pulumi-lang-go="`getWorkerCatalog`" pulumi-lang-python="`get_worker_catalog`" pulumi-lang-yaml="`agentops.getWorkerCatalog`" pulumi-lang-java="`agentops.getWorkerCatalog`" pulumi-lang-hcl="`data.agentops_worker_catalog`">`agentops.getWorkerCatalog`</span> data source) and the token is supplied separately.

**Authentication:** the self-hosted deploy endpoint requires a user-bound API key (a PAT). A service-account token is rejected with <span pulumi-lang-nodejs="`403`" pulumi-lang-dotnet="`403`" pulumi-lang-go="`403`" pulumi-lang-python="`403`" pulumi-lang-yaml="`403`" pulumi-lang-java="`403`" pulumi-lang-hcl="`403`">`403`</span>, so configure the provider with a personal API key for this resource.

Deploy-time inputs are write-only (the API does not return them), so out-of-band changes to them are not detected, and changing any of them forces a new deployment — which mints a **new** worker token and invalidates the secret already applied to the cluster. This resource does not support import: the originating <span pulumi-lang-nodejs="`catalogId`" pulumi-lang-dotnet="`CatalogId`" pulumi-lang-go="`catalogId`" pulumi-lang-python="`catalog_id`" pulumi-lang-yaml="`catalogId`" pulumi-lang-java="`catalogId`" pulumi-lang-hcl="`catalog_id`">`catalogId`</span> and the once-only token cannot be recovered from the API.

**Destroying this resource — and any change that forces a new one — needs the <span pulumi-lang-nodejs="`selfHostedAgentLifecycle`" pulumi-lang-dotnet="`SelfHostedAgentLifecycle`" pulumi-lang-go="`selfHostedAgentLifecycle`" pulumi-lang-python="`self_hosted_agent_lifecycle`" pulumi-lang-yaml="`selfHostedAgentLifecycle`" pulumi-lang-java="`selfHostedAgentLifecycle`" pulumi-lang-hcl="`self_hosted_agent_lifecycle`">`selfHostedAgentLifecycle`</span> feature enabled for your AgentOps account once the agent's worker has come online.** The feature is off by default. Without it `DELETE /agents/{id}` is refused, and the provider falls back to the ungated delete, which the control plane serves only for an agent that has never heartbeated. So a registered-but-never-deployed agent destroys cleanly on any account, while one whose worker has heartbeated fails the destroy — and, because every replacement destroys before it creates, fails the replacing apply too, leaving the old agent archived and the new one uncreated. Ask Komodor support or your account admin to enable the feature before tearing down or replacing a deployed agent.

## Example Usage

<!--Start PulumiCodeChooser -->
```typescript
import * as pulumi from "@pulumi/pulumi";
import * as agentops from "@pulumi/agentops";
import * as kubernetes from "@pulumi/kubernetes";

// Deploy a curated worker from the catalog into your own cluster. The control
// plane registers the agent and mints its worker token; you run the container.
// Browse available entries (and their IDs / required credentials) via the
// agentops_worker_catalog data source.
//
// The endpoint requires a user-bound API key (a PAT) — a service-account token
// is rejected.
const k8sTroubleshooter = new agentops.SelfHostedCatalogDeployment("k8s_troubleshooter", {
    catalogId: "k8s-troubleshooter",
    agentId: "prod-k8s-troubleshooter",
    displayName: "Prod K8s Troubleshooter (self-hosted)",
    credentialRef: "anthropic-api-key",
    integrationConnections: {
        aws: "conn_01hxyz",
    },
});
// The token is returned once, at deploy time. Feed it to the worker as
// AGENTOPS_WORKER_TOKEN — here via a Kubernetes secret the agentops-agent-base
// chart consumes.
const workerToken = new kubernetes.core.v1.Secret("worker_token", {
    metadata: {
        name: "prod-k8s-troubleshooter-token",
        namespace: "agentops",
    },
    data: {
        AGENTOPS_WORKER_TOKEN: k8sTroubleshooter.token,
    },
});
